WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.736 --> 00:00:04.760
[INAUDIBLE].

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.660
[BEEPING]

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.660
We're here, yeah.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.640
We're in Valdez.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.553
It is 7:35.

00:00:15.553 --> 00:00:17.698
We're five minutes behind.

00:00:17.698 --> 00:00:22.100
Um, bluebird morning-- we
got some snow yesterday.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.423
Gonna ride some lines
and do some flips.

00:00:26.423 --> 00:00:27.590
It's going to be a good day.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.490
[HELICOPTER ENGINE REVVING]

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.535
I was up in Alaska with my
crew working on this film,

00:00:40.535 --> 00:00:41.435
"The Fourth Phase."

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.770
I think let's do this
wizard thing real quick.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.874
It looks extremely good.

00:00:48.874 --> 00:00:52.746
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:52.746 --> 00:00:55.990
MAN (VOICEOVER): You need a
canvas to put your work on.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.200
And the type of art
that I like to make

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.840
demands a very large canvas.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.965
And that is Alaska.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.850
You find snow features in
Alaska that you don't find

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.560
anywhere else in the world.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:20.070
On my first couple trips,
I was really scared.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.400
And scared not just on
a, I might get hurt,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.770
but like there was
a death component.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.690
You have cornices on the ridges.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.880
You have crevasses.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.100
There's a lot of big cliffs.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.880
You get out of a helicopter
on top of a ridge.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.140
And you look a couple
thousand feet down,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.880
and there's an absolute
moment of leap of faith,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.820
and then drop in and
surrender to the unknown.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.920
Everything in your body is
like, speed check, speed check.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.120
You're going too fast.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.734
Slow down.

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.150
And you can't.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:17.570
You have to just try to override
the body's natural ability

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.470
to protect itself.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.732
[CHUCKLING]

00:02:19.732 --> 00:02:23.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:27.350
Avalanches, of course, are
a constant thing that you're

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:28.520
thinking and talking about.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.830
MAN: I've been skiing in the
big mountains for over 25 years.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.630
And I've seen a lot
of bad things go down.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.190
If you get caught
in an avalanche

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:46.864
on a big Alaskan face, you're
not stopping until the bottom.

00:02:46.864 --> 00:02:49.560
MAN: All right, just do
a little snow check here.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.300
OK.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.620
With all these dangers,
why put yourself out there?

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.550
For me, riding in Alaska, it's
the art that I have worked

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.260
towards for so many years.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.900
And all of the little
subtleties and the nuances

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.520
and the details, like,
that's where it matters.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

